# ThreadSanitizer suppressions. Used by tools/tsan.sh; see docs/LOCKING.md for the design these
# describe. Keep this file short: every entry hides real reports as well as false ones, so an entry
# must name a protocol that TSan structurally cannot understand, not a race we have not got round to.

# ProcessDataRing is a sequence lock, and TSan cannot see a sequence lock.
#
# The RT writer invalidates a slot's sequence word, memcpy's the payload, then release-stores the
# real sequence. A reader checks the sequence, copies, and re-checks after an acquire fence — a
# reader that was lapped mid-copy detects it and discards what it read. So the racing payload copies
# are the mechanism, not a mistake: the reader's bytes are known to be untrustworthy until the second
# check clears them.
#
# TSan sees two unsynchronised memcpys over the same bytes and reports six races per run. It has no
# way to learn that the reader throws the result away. The alternative — copying the payload as
# relaxed atomic words on both sides — would make the model strictly conformant at some cost on the
# hottest RT write path; it is worth measuring before it is worth doing, and it is recorded in
# NEXTGEN.md rather than done here.
race:mm::node::ProcessDataRing::write
race:mm::node::ProcessDataRing::readRecord
